{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"519095e5cfa6b4a47054b6b640cd8c977dfbaa5974fddf4c0b4d03f3335f6126"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 misidentifies the 'precise distinction' by treating the RFC 9110 §9.2.1 client-expectation language ('the client does not request, and does not expect, any state change') as a separate axis beyond the simple 'safe = read-only, idempotent = convergent under repetition' framing. The RFC's client-expectation clause is its way of *defining* 'read-only' — it states that no state change is intended or expected — not an additional dimension that distinguishes safety from idempotence. The genuine precise distinction remains: safe methods produce no server state change at all, while idempotent methods may change state but are invariant under repetition (RFC 9110 §9.2.2). By claiming the distinction is 'not merely' this simple framing but 'rather' the client-expectation dimension, SRC_002 obscures the actual axis of distinction (presence vs. absence of state change) and substitutes a restatement of safety's own definition as if it were a new distinguishing criterion. The conclusion about the event log requiring idempotence is correct, but the reasoning path to it is overcomplicated and imprecise about what separates the two properties.","counterexample":[{"section":"RFC 9110 §9.2.1","text":"a request method is considered 'safe' if its defined semantics are essentially read-only"},{"section":"RFC 9110 §9.2.2","text":"A request method is considered 'idempotent' if the intended effect on the server of multiple identical requests with that method is the same as the effect for a single such request."}],"target":"8e394f49e7f08af01a5485db4afa96c57bd95d7bd742d77f6a1b22f4c002c8ef"}]}